---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="About" description="How the Atlas of Justice works, where the drawings come from and how to add a case.">
  <section class="about">
    <h1>About the Atlas</h1>
    <p>
      The Atlas collects trials and hearings from many countries. Each case has a place on the map, a short
      description, the outcome and a list of sources. Cameras are often barred from courtrooms, so for many
      famous cases the public knew the proceedings through the drawings of court artists.
    </p>

    <h2>The drawings</h2>
    <p>
      A drawing appears here only when its rights status can be confirmed at the source. Two kinds qualify:
      works the Wikimedia Commons marks as public domain, and items the Library of Congress lists with the rights
      advisory "No known restrictions on publication". The second is the Library's own statement, not a legal
      guarantee, so every drawing is shown with its artist, rights note and a link back to the archive record.
      Where no drawing has been cleared, the case page shows an empty frame instead. Much modern courtroom art is
      still under copyright, which is why many cases are missing a picture.
    </p>

    <h2>The map</h2>
    <p>
      A pin marks the city or district of the court, not a street address. Base map: OpenStreetMap contributors.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding a case</h2>
    <p>
      Each case is one Markdown file in <code>src/content/cases/</code>: data at the top, the write-up below. To add a
      drawing, list it in <code>data/sketches.json</code>; the script <code>scripts/fetch-sketches.mjs</code> checks the
      rights statement and downloads it during the build.
    </p>
    <pre><code>---
title: Name of the proceeding
country: Country
city: City
court: Name of the court
region: europe        # europe, north-america, south-america, africa, asia, oceania
category: political   # international, transitional, political, miscarriage, society, hearing
lat: 48.85
lng: 2.35
yearStart: 1899
yearEnd: 1906         # optional
summary: One or two sentences for the card.
outcome: How it ended.
sources:
  - title: Source title
    url: https://example.org/source
---

Two or three paragraphs of description.</code></pre>
  </section>
</Base>
